with the earlier low horizon, later low horizon, and final high all active. Iteration 18 promotes the full-to-reduced LP for one drought, so this is no longer merely a reduced-model warning. Any closed form for unequal lows must account for front-loading and earlier low constraints; the equal-low last-low formula is not a pooling-free template.

## CE-026: Three-Low Active-Set Stress Test For Pooling Rules

Status: accepted reduced-LP regression test; not a counterexample to all revisable active-set methods.

For the one-drought path
\[
0,\ 0.2,\ 0^8,\ 0.3,\ 0,\ 0.4,\ 1
\]
in the formal left-limit model, the CL-049 reduced LP has
\[
S=(0.2,3,4.8),\qquad B=(1,9,2),
\]
and certified value
\[
z=291/100.
\]
One primal certificate is
\[
x_{0,0.4}=1,\qquad x_{1,0.3}=0.3,\qquad
x_{1,0.4}=1.5,\qquad x_{2,0.4}=2.
\]
The dual certificate
\[
\alpha_1=0,\qquad \alpha_2=1/4,\qquad \alpha_3=9/20,\qquad \beta=3/10
\]
has \(\sum_j\alpha_j+\beta=1\), objective \(291/100\), and \(\psi_0=\psi_1=\psi_2=0\). The low deficits are \(0.2,2.91,2.91\), and the final-high debt is \(2.91\).

This stresses proposed pooling rules. The first low \(0.2\) is slack, while lows \(0.3\) and \(0.4\) satisfy an equal-revenue-like active relation:
\[
\alpha_2+\alpha_3=0.7=\beta(1/0.3-1),
\qquad
\alpha_3=0.45=\beta(1/0.4-1).
\]
Thus a greedy rule that permanently finalizes the first adjacent pair is wrong. A revisable PAVA-style or active-set method remains possible, but it must produce primal and dual certificates and handle running-max constraints, segment capacities, and pressure terms.

